WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:03.870
Joe Biden is promising return to normalcy
in U.S. foreign policy,

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.970
following the turbulent Trump years.

00:00:05.970 --> 00:00:10.090
The Biden foreign policy agenda will place America back at the head of the table,

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:14.820
working with our allies and our partners to mobilize global action on global threats.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.750
But be careful what you wish for.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:22.300
Saddam Hussein is still seeking the accumulation of weapons of mass destruction.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:27.908
Biden supported the wars in Iraq and Syria, and the drone bombing campaign in Pakistan.

00:00:27.908 --> 00:00:33.750
In the 1990s, he co-sponsored a bill with John McCain, pushing the U.S. to bomb Kosovo.

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.810
He advocated a troop surge in Afghanistan before he later opposed it.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:41.079
Trump broke with America's foreign policy establishment.

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:46.120
It all began with the dangerous idea that we could make Western democracies

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:52.750
out of countries that had no experience or interest in becoming a Western democracy.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.340
And though he talked the big game, "great nations do not fight endless wars,"

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:02.739
and has even installed a Pentagon adviser in the waning months of his presidency

00:01:02.739 --> 00:01:08.080
to accelerate troop withdrawal from Afghanistan, thousands of troops will likely remain there,

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.021
and in Iraq, Syria, and elsewhere in the Middle East, at the end of his term.

00:01:12.021 --> 00:01:17.100
Biden on the other hand, embodies the establishment view that the U.S. must police the world

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:21.990
because "building and maintaining the post World War II International Order

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:27.509
is what made America the indispensable, unquestionable, leader of the free world.

00:01:27.509 --> 00:01:29.549
And in turn, protecting us."

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:34.779
A Biden presidency might entail a return to the Obama era policies of aerial warfare,

00:01:34.779 --> 00:01:39.380
heavy reliance on drone strikes, and maybe even a willingness to enter a multi-national

00:01:39.380 --> 00:01:42.759
military conflicts without seeking congressional approval.

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:48.331
Such as the Libya intervention that toppled Muammar Gaddafi and proceeded a bloody civil war.

00:01:48.331 --> 00:01:52.229
We came, we saw, he died.

00:01:52.229 --> 00:01:56.440
In Washington D.C., there's a saying, "Personnel is policy."

00:01:56.440 --> 00:02:00.139
And new administrations often draw from think tanks for both.

00:02:00.139 --> 00:02:05.170
Trump's more establishment personnel fought to constrain his non-interventionist impulses.

00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:07.610
There were high profile resignations,

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:12.840
and one outgoing diplomat even admitted to lying about troop levels in Northern Syria

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:15.000
after Trump had ordered a withdrawal.

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:18.911
Biden is reportedly eyeing Susan Rice, for Secretary of State.

00:02:18.911 --> 00:02:23.150
For Defense Secretary, Biden is rumored to be considering Michele Flournoy,

00:02:23.150 --> 00:02:27.970
an Obama administration alumna, who co-founded the Center for a New American Security,

00:02:27.970 --> 00:02:33.060
an influential think tank whose experts emphasize the need for more military preparedness

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.769
against China and Russia, and a smaller but ongoing U.S. presence in the Middle East.

00:02:38.769 --> 00:02:42.330
In terms of a Biden foreign policy, we know who Joe Biden is.

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:46.969
He's very much, historically, sort of ascribed to kind of liberal interventionism,

00:02:46.969 --> 00:02:53.600
and this notion that the U.S. needs to take a big role in the international stage for the good of the world.

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:59.070
Annelle Sheline is a foreign policy analyst at the Quincy Institute for Responsible Statecraft,

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.110
formed in December of 2019,

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:05.920
with seed funding from the foundations of both progressive billionaire, George Soros,

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.350
and libertarian billionaire, Charles Koch.

00:03:08.350 --> 00:03:14.550
It's a different kind of foreign policy think tank that aims to cultivate a roster of foreign policy experts,

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:18.800
who can guide future administrations towards military restraint.

00:03:18.800 --> 00:03:22.320
It's named for the former American President, John Quincy Adams,

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.210
who once said that, "America goes not abroad in search of monsters to destroy."

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:32.080
While it's a famous quote, I think parts of it have really been forgotten or neglected.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:36.580
It's not just because it would be very bad in the sense that we would be overextended

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.750
and would lead to forever wars.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.892
It's also because of how it will change America internally,

00:03:41.892 --> 00:03:46.234
which he says, America can become the dictatress of the world, 

00:03:46.234 --> 00:03:49.450
but in doing so, she will lose her own spirit and her own freedom.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:50.730
I think we have seen that.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:57.090
As we have gone abroad in more and more wars, the security state as home has also become stronger and stronger.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:00.160
And many of the liberties that the American people have traditionally faced,

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:04.470
seem as seen as absolutely uncompromising, have been compromised.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:08.250
Trita Parsi is the executive vice president at the Quincy Institute.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:14.690
The vast majority of think tanks in D.C. do not question the first principles of American foreign policy.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:20.450
Quincy is deliberately different in that sense, because we believe that these endless wars

00:04:20.450 --> 00:04:25.990
that the United States has ended up in, is a direct consequence of the U.S.'s grand strategy

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:28.880
of primacy or global military domination.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:34.450
The Quincy Institute argues that the U.S. should abandon that strategy of global military domination

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:39.810
and accept the reality of multi-polarity or competing regional influences.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:44.510
The longer that the United States continues to behave in ways that

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:50.700
were more possible when it truly was the world's only super power,

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:54.350
are just going to continue to fritter away American resources,

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:57.650
which really, obviously, need to be spent here at home.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:01.320
One of the Institute's core tenants is that American foreign policy

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.810
should derive directly from U.S. interests rigorously defined.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.760
The United States needs to focus on protecting the territory of the United States itself,

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:11.990
and the people that are here.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.320
One of the major bad assumptions driving U.S. foreign policy

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:21.980
is the notion that the U.S. presence in the Middle East makes the United States more safe.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.310
And this is fundamentally untrue.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:28.008
Biden plans to keep troops in Afghanistan indefinitely.

00:05:28.008 --> 00:05:32.861
You do think there should be some U.S. presence that remains in Afghanistan? Yes, a very small presence

00:05:32.861 --> 00:05:36.469
to be able to determine whether or not I mean, a small footprint.

00:05:36.469 --> 00:05:40.620
What does that look like? It looks like several thousand people.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:44.810
Sheline says America doesn't need troops on the ground in the Middle East at all,

00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.860
because a Naval presence is sufficient to safeguard America's main strategic interests,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:54.421
which are keeping trade and travel routes open and preventing the rise of a regional hegemon

00:05:54.421 --> 00:05:56.900
that could threaten U.S. national security.

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:02.191
There's really no actor in the Middle East currently that would be able to take on that role.

00:06:02.191 --> 00:06:05.440
She says China has learned from U.S. military misadventures

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:09.240
and seems unlikely to get involved militarily in the Middle East,

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:12.370
while Iran is too weak to become the dominant force,

00:06:12.370 --> 00:06:16.859
and Russia operates through a series of self-serving temporary partnerships.

00:06:16.859 --> 00:06:22.231
The U.S. would do well to take a page out of Russia's book and to say, "Look, Saudi Arabia,

00:06:22.231 --> 00:06:25.210
you're not acting in a way that is supporting our interests.

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:30.459
Maybe we will go talk to Iran, and we shouldn't be so afraid to reach out to them."

00:06:30.459 --> 00:06:34.300
To stop Iran's path to nuclear weapons and missiles,

00:06:34.300 --> 00:06:39.520
I withdrew the United States from the terrible Iran Nuclear Deal.

00:06:39.520 --> 00:06:44.200
One aspect of Biden's foreign policy stance that the Quincy Institute agrees with,

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:49.210
is the president-elect's pledged to reenter the Iran Deal that Trump walked away from.

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:53.470
The Iran Deal was not only accomplishing a critical mission,

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:57.859
it also created an environment where diplomacy was possible.

00:06:57.859 --> 00:07:00.400
But Trump, he walked away.

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:02.000
I thought the nuclear deal was actually very good

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.720
because it prevented a war with Iran,

00:07:03.720 --> 00:07:07.490
and prevented the Iranis from being able to have a pathway to a nuclear bomb.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:13.010
Once Trump pulled out, Iran began enriching uranium at 10 times the rate allowed under the deal,

00:07:13.010 --> 00:07:14.011
according to Parsi.

00:07:14.011 --> 00:07:18.101
Sheline says that although Saudi Arabia and Israel have a stake in the U.S.

00:07:18.101 --> 00:07:23.241
continuing to treat Iran as an enemy, it doesn't pose a significant threat to America.

00:07:23.241 --> 00:07:26.240
Iran's GDP is smaller than New Jersey.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:32.330
This ongoing animosity between the U.S. and Iran doesn't really make a whole lot of sense.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:36.800
As U.S. troops leave the Middle East, Parsi suggests America encourage countries

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:39.810
to form a regional security organization,

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:43.310
but that it's crucial for the U.S. not to take a leading role in it.

00:07:43.310 --> 00:07:46.150
This is the instinct a lot of people have in this town.

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:49.120
That whatever we do, we have to be in the lead of it.

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:52.260
It will take agency away from the regional countries,

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:56.430
and ultimately, it's the regional countries that will benefit from the security architecture.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:08:00.740
Their incentives to move in this direction should be stronger than ours.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:05.960
While the Quincy Institute is unlikely to be the go-to foreign policy think tank of a Biden administration,

00:08:05.960 --> 00:08:09.050
with its deep ties to the foreign policy establishment,

00:08:09.050 --> 00:08:12.990
Parsi says more politicians and bureaucrats will eventually come around to their vision

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:17.300
for a more restrained foreign policy, quite possibly out of necessity.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:21.389
And that when it happens, the Quincy Institute will have the personnel at the ready

00:08:21.389 --> 00:08:24.869
to make the pivot away from interventionism, a reality.

00:08:24.869 --> 00:08:29.121
I'll be frank. I think what we are calling for, inevitably will happen.

00:08:29.121 --> 00:08:32.560
Because we are bleeding ourselves to death with these endless wars.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:36.510
And at one point, it's just not going to be possible for us to continue doing so.

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:42.024
We want the shift to be a proactive shift, that is done before we have weakened ourselves too much.

